From ef7b57d8bfcc1f7c6a7c86809fb7d2d5fa49345f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chen Xiaohui <graych@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 3 May 2026 22:02:50 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] vmm: snapshot: skip user-managed memory zones
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Memory zones whose backing fd was injected via the previous commit's
`--memory-zone fd=...` option are owned by the parent process. The
parent has direct access to the same shmem inode and is the
authoritative source for save/restore of those bytes — CH must not
duplicate them through /vm.snapshot's memory_ranges path.

This is the same shape as the existing hardlink-shared-file fast
path used for file-backed zones (where `region.file_offset()` carries
the original on-disk offset and CH skips the dump). The hardlink
trick can't apply to a memfd because `st_nlink == 0` for memfd
inodes, hence the explicit `user_managed` flag.

Behaviour
=========
A new `user_managed: bool` field on MemoryZone records whether the
zone was constructed from an inherited fd. memory_range_table sets
`continue` on `snapshot && memory_zone.user_managed()`, so the
returned MemoryRangeTable contains only CH-owned ranges.

/vm.snapshot still completes successfully and returns a valid
memory_ranges list. The user-managed bytes are captured by the host
out-of-band (typically by reading the same memfd and walking
SEEK_DATA/HOLE for sparseness).

Mixed configurations work transparently: zones without fd= are
dumped normally; zones with fd= are skipped en bloc.

Backwards compat
================
Without `--memory-zone fd=...` no zone is ever flagged user_managed,
so memory_range_table behaviour is identical to upstream.

Signed-off-by: Chen Xiaohui <graych@gmail.com>
